pling based on lateral G-forces to chase and onboard cameras for realistic cornering behavior, limiting the bank to +/- 2.2 degrees to avoid unrealistic roll. Added deterministic micro-vibration based on speed and kerb/corner proximity.\n\n\nFine-tuned camera micro-vibrations across several viewpoints for a realistic simulation of suspension and road surface detail. The focus was on controlled, non-nauseating visuals, particularly for cameras such as `S02_Onboard`, `S09_WheelMacro`, and `S19_Onboard3`.\n\nAddressing persistent camera occlusion issues, repositioned several cameras to ensure clear visibility. Added an automated occlusion self-check within the animation script, using raycasting to verify camera-to-bone visibility throughout the animation frames. This automated check is designed to scan for any frame-level obstructions, ensuring comprehensive shot integrity.\n\n\nInitial focus is on the `pipeline/v5_clay.py` script, specifically addressing classification accuracy as defined in Brief #3 and verifier item 9. The objective is to ensure precise identification of car and environment elements for clay rendering, explicitly verifying object classification based on parentage, naming conventions, and excluding specific prefixes to eliminate incorrect material assignments. Maintaining DOF and motion blur settings is also critical.\n\n\nObserved that the existing script was not correctly handling the exposure override during clay rendering. Identified that keyframes in the scene were being overwritten, leading to incorrect exposure values and highlight clipping. A solution is needed to preserve the animation's fade-out while implementing the desired exposure for clay rendering. The goal is to either utilize a different view transform or adjust the exposure settings directly.\n\nThe `Standard` view transform is being considered as an option, where manual adjustments will be made. Initial testing of a tuned exposure value is planned with image processing to ensure highlight clipping remains under 0.5% and proper clay brightness is achieved. The final strategy will include the scene's animation data to preserve the end fade-out.\n\n\nCareful analysis suggests that adjusting the exposure settings will resolve the clay rendering issue without altering the original animation's fade-out. Testing confirms that setting the view transform to 'Standard', a look of 'None', and manually setting the exposure value to -0.35, along with updating f-curve keyframes, preserves the animation while eliminating highlight clipping.\n\nThe next step is to add an optional flag to the script. This flag, if enabled, should render six representative clay stills after generating the main file.\n\n\nCorrecting the `pipeline/v5_viewport_fast.py` script to meet the specified requirements, specifically regarding object display types and collection visibility, ensures efficient viewport performance. The `HEAVY_OBJ_PREFIX` list will be expanded to encompass all heavy environment prefixes, and `ob.display_type` will only be set to `BOUNDS` for objects of type `MESH`. The `HEAVY_COLLS` list, which determines the hidden collections, will match the defined parameters.\n\n\nEmpties require careful handling, specifically those starting with \"V5_\" or \"EM_\"; these will not receive a bounds display type and their display size must remain compact or be hidden via their collection. This avoids unexpected behavior due to invalid display types. The script will explicitly save the `.blend` file after execution when a file path is provided, unless a `--no-save` flag is used, because the integration tests require that a subsequent run leaves the scene in the \"normal\" mode. The `v5_stills.py` script requires updating to render specified stills and generate a contact sheet, as per the current brief.\n\n\nThe `v5_stills.py` script will support two modes. In standalone Python mode, it copies frames from a specified directory to generate the contact sheet. Blender's render 